const FAM_WEEKS={"46": {"t": "“There Is No Other God That Can Deliver”", "b": "Daniel 1–7", "u": "https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/manual/come-follow-me-for-home-and-church-old-testament-2026/46?lang=eng"}, "47": {"t": "“I Will Love Them Freely”", "b": "Hosea 1–6; 10–14; Joel", "u": "https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/manual/come-follow-me-for-home-and-church-old-testament-2026/47?lang=eng"}, "48": {"t": "“Seek the Lord, and Ye Shall Live”", "b": "Amos; Obadiah; Jonah", "u": "https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/study/manual/come-follow-me-for-home-and-church-old-testament-2026/48?lang=eng"}};
const FAM=[{"d": "2026-11-15", "w": 46, "t": "Daniel keeps praying", "a": [{"r": "Daniel 6:10, 22", "v": [[6, 10, "Now when Daniel knew that the writing was signed, he went into his house; and his windows being open in his chamber toward Jerusalem, he kneeled upon his knees three times a day, and prayed, and gave thanks before his God, as he did aforetime."], [6, 22, "My God hath sent his angel, and hath shut the lions’ mouths, that they have not hurt me: forasmuch as before him innocency was found in me; and also before thee, O king, have I done no hurt."]], "q": "Daniel prayed three times a day, even when the king made a rule against it. When are times you can pray?", "act": "Take turns kneeling the way Daniel did, then have a child say the family prayer."}, {"r": "Daniel 6:10, 16, 19–22", "v": [[6, 10, "Now when Daniel knew that the writing was signed, he went into his house; and his windows being open in his chamber toward Jerusalem, he kneeled upon his knees three times a day, and prayed, and gave thanks before his God, as he did aforetime."], [6, 16, "Then the king commanded, and they brought Daniel, and cast him into the den of lions. Now the king spake and said unto Daniel, Thy God whom thou servest continually, he will deliver thee."], [6, 19, "Then the king arose very early in the morning, and went in haste unto the den of lions."], [6, 20, "And when he came to the den, he cried with a lamentable voice unto Daniel: and the king spake and said to Daniel, O Daniel, servant of the living God, is thy God, whom thou servest continually, able to deliver thee from the lions?"], [6, 21, "Then said Daniel unto the king, O king, live for ever."], [6, 22, "My God hath sent his angel, and hath shut the lions’ mouths, that they have not hurt me: forasmuch as before him innocency was found in me; and also before thee, O king, have I done no hurt."]], "q": "Daniel knew about the new law and still prayed with his windows open. Why do you think he didn't hide?"}, {"r": "Daniel 6:4–10, 16–23", "v": [[6, 4, "Then the presidents and princes sought to find occasion against Daniel concerning the kingdom; but they could find none occasion nor fault; forasmuch as he was faithful, neither was there any error or fault found in him."], [6, 5, "Then said these men, We shall not find any occasion against this Daniel, except we find it against him concerning the law of his God."], [6, 6, "Then these presidents and princes assembled together to the king, and said thus unto him, King Darius, live for ever."], [6, 7, "All the presidents of the kingdom, the governors, and the princes, the counsellors, and the captains, have consulted together to establish a royal statute, and to make a firm decree, that whosoever shall ask a petition of any God or man for thirty days, save of thee, O king, he shall be cast into the den of lions."], [6, 8, "Now, O king, establish the decree, and sign the writing, that it be not changed, according to the law of the Medes and Persians, which altereth not."], [6, 9, "Wherefore king Darius signed the writing and the decree."], [6, 10, "Now when Daniel knew that the writing was signed, he went into his house; and his windows being open in his chamber toward Jerusalem, he kneeled upon his knees three times a day, and prayed, and gave thanks before his God, as he did aforetime."], [6, 16, "Then the king commanded, and they brought Daniel, and cast him into the den of lions. Now the king spake and said unto Daniel, Thy God whom thou servest continually, he will deliver thee."], [6, 17, "And a stone was brought, and laid upon the mouth of the den; and the king sealed it with his own signet, and with the signet of his lords; that the purpose might not be changed concerning Daniel."], [6, 18, "Then the king went to his palace, and passed the night fasting: neither were instruments of musick brought before him: and his sleep went from him."], [6, 19, "Then the king arose very early in the morning, and went in haste unto the den of lions."], [6, 20, "And when he came to the den, he cried with a lamentable voice unto Daniel: and the king spake and said to Daniel, O Daniel, servant of the living God, is thy God, whom thou servest continually, able to deliver thee from the lions?"], [6, 21, "Then said Daniel unto the king, O king, live for ever."], [6, 22, "My God hath sent his angel, and hath shut the lions’ mouths, that they have not hurt me: forasmuch as before him innocency was found in me; and also before thee, O king, have I done no hurt."], [6, 23, "Then was the king exceeding glad for him, and commanded that they should take Daniel up out of the den. So Daniel was taken up out of the den, and no manner of hurt was found upon him, because he believed in his God."]], "q": "His enemies said the only way to catch Daniel was through “the law of his God” (verse 5). What does that tell you about how he lived?"}, {"r": "Daniel 6:1–23", "v": [[6, 1, "It pleased Darius to set over the kingdom an hundred and twenty princes, which should be over the whole kingdom;"], [6, 2, "And over these three presidents; of whom Daniel was first: that the princes might give accounts unto them, and the king should have no damage."], [6, 3, "Then this Daniel was preferred above the presidents and princes, because an excellent spirit was in him; and the king thought to set him over the whole realm."], [6, 4, "Then the presidents and princes sought to find occasion against Daniel concerning the kingdom; but they could find none occasion nor fault; forasmuch as he was faithful, neither was there any error or fault found in him."], [6, 5, "Then said these men, We shall not find any occasion against this Daniel, except we find it against him concerning the law of his God."], [6, 6, "Then these presidents and princes assembled together to the king, and said thus unto him, King Darius, live for ever."], [6, 7, "All the presidents of the kingdom, the governors, and the princes, the counsellors, and the captains, have consulted together to establish a royal statute, and to make a firm decree, that whosoever shall ask a petition of any God or man for thirty days, save of thee, O king, he shall be cast into the den of lions."], [6, 8, "Now, O king, establish the decree, and sign the writing, that it be not changed, according to the law of the Medes and Persians, which altereth not."], [6, 9, "Wherefore king Darius signed the writing and the decree."], [6, 10, "Now when Daniel knew that the writing was signed, he went into his house; and his windows being open in his chamber toward Jerusalem, he kneeled upon his knees three times a day, and prayed, and gave thanks before his God, as he did aforetime."], [6, 11, "Then these men assembled, and found Daniel praying and making supplication before his God."], [6, 12, "Then they came near, and spake before the king concerning the king’s decree; Hast thou not signed a decree, that every man that shall ask a petition of any God or man within thirty days, save of thee, O king, shall be cast into the den of lions? The king answered and said, The thing is true, according to the law of the Medes and Persians, which altereth not."], [6, 13, "Then answered they and said before the king, That Daniel, which is of the children of the captivity of Judah, regardeth not thee, O king, nor the decree that thou hast signed, but maketh his petition three times a day."], [6, 14, "Then the king, when he heard these words, was sore displeased with himself, and set his heart on Daniel to deliver him: and he laboured till the going down of the sun to deliver him."], [6, 15, "Then these men assembled unto the king, and said unto the king, Know, O king, that the law of the Medes and Persians is, That no decree nor statute which the king establisheth may be changed."], [6, 16, "Then the king commanded, and they brought Daniel, and cast him into the den of lions. Now the king spake and said unto Daniel, Thy God whom thou servest continually, he will deliver thee."], [6, 17, "And a stone was brought, and laid upon the mouth of the den; and the king sealed it with his own signet, and with the signet of his lords; that the purpose might not be changed concerning Daniel."], [6, 18, "Then the king went to his palace, and passed the night fasting: neither were instruments of musick brought before him: and his sleep went from him."], [6, 19, "Then the king arose very early in the morning, and went in haste unto the den of lions."], [6, 20, "And when he came to the den, he cried with a lamentable voice unto Daniel: and the king spake and said to Daniel, O Daniel, servant of the living God, is thy God, whom thou servest continually, able to deliver thee from the lions?"], [6, 21, "Then said Daniel unto the king, O king, live for ever."], [6, 22, "My God hath sent his angel, and hath shut the lions’ mouths, that they have not hurt me: forasmuch as before him innocency was found in me; and also before thee, O king, have I done no hurt."], [6, 23, "Then was the king exceeding glad for him, and commanded that they should take Daniel up out of the den. So Daniel was taken up out of the den, and no manner of hurt was found upon him, because he believed in his God."]], "q": "Daniel prayed “as he did aforetime” (verse 10). The habit was already there before the crisis came. Which habits keep your family steady when pressure comes?"}], "ck": "Daniel is an old man now, and a new king named Darius rules. Some jealous leaders trick the king into making a law against praying to anyone but him.", "cf": "Babylon has fallen to the Medes and Persians (539 BC), and Daniel, now in his 80s, serves King Darius. Jealous officials can't find any fault in his work, so they target his faith with a law that, once signed, can't be changed.", "wd": ["den", "גֹּב", "gob", "Aramaic", "a pit. Lions were kept in a pit in the ground, and a stone was rolled over the opening (verse 17)."]}, {"d": "2026-11-16", "w": 47, "t": "God loves us freely", "a": [{"r": "Hosea 14:4", "v": [[14, 4, "I will heal their backsliding, I will love them freely: for mine anger is turned away from him."]], "q": "God says, “I will love them freely.” What does it feel like when someone loves you even after you make a mistake?", "act": "Go around the room and give each person a hug and say, “I love you, no matter what.”"}, {"r": "Hosea 14:1–2, 4", "v": [[14, 1, "O Israel, return unto the LORD thy God; for thou hast fallen by thine iniquity."], [14, 2, "Take with you words, and turn to the LORD: say unto him, Take away all iniquity, and receive us graciously: so will we render the calves of our lips."], [14, 4, "I will heal their backsliding, I will love them freely: for mine anger is turned away from him."]], "q": "God asks Israel to “return” to Him. What does it mean to come back to God after we do something wrong?"}, {"r": "Hosea 14:1–4", "v": [[14, 1, "O Israel, return unto the LORD thy God; for thou hast fallen by thine iniquity."], [14, 2, "Take with you words, and turn to the LORD: say unto him, Take away all iniquity, and receive us graciously: so will we render the calves of our lips."], [14, 3, "Asshur shall not save us; we will not ride upon horses: neither will we say any more to the work of our hands, Ye are our gods: for in thee the fatherless findeth mercy."], [14, 4, "I will heal their backsliding, I will love them freely: for mine anger is turned away from him."]], "q": "Verse 2 says to “take with you words” when you turn back to the Lord. What words would you say to Him?"}, {"r": "Hosea 14:1–9", "v": [[14, 1, "O Israel, return unto the LORD thy God; for thou hast fallen by thine iniquity."], [14, 2, "Take with you words, and turn to the LORD: say unto him, Take away all iniquity, and receive us graciously: so will we render the calves of our lips."], [14, 3, "Asshur shall not save us; we will not ride upon horses: neither will we say any more to the work of our hands, Ye are our gods: for in thee the fatherless findeth mercy."], [14, 4, "I will heal their backsliding, I will love them freely: for mine anger is turned away from him."], [14, 5, "I will be as the dew unto Israel: he shall grow as the lily, and cast forth his roots as Lebanon."], [14, 6, "His branches shall spread, and his beauty shall be as the olive tree, and his smell as Lebanon."], [14, 7, "They that dwell under his shadow shall return; they shall revive as the corn, and grow as the vine: the scent thereof shall be as the wine of Lebanon."], [14, 8, "Ephraim shall say, What have I to do any more with idols? I have heard him, and observed him: I am like a green fir tree. From me is thy fruit found."], [14, 9, "Who is wise, and he shall understand these things? prudent, and he shall know them? for the ways of the LORD are right, and the just shall walk in them: but the transgressors shall fall therein."]], "q": "Hosea 14 promises healing, not just forgiveness (verses 4–7). What pictures does God use, and what do they say about how He helps us change?"}], "ck": "Hosea lived in Israel long ago. His people kept turning away from God, but God kept inviting them to come back.", "cf": "Hosea preached in the northern kingdom of Israel around 750–725 BC, shortly before Assyria conquered it in 722 BC. God had him marry Gomer, who left him; Hosea took her back. His marriage became a picture of how God keeps reaching for His people.", "wd": ["return", "שׁוּב", "shuv", "Hebrew", "to turn around and come back. It's the Old Testament's main word for repenting."]}, {"d": "2026-11-17", "w": 47, "t": "Keeping our promises with God", "a": [{"r": "Hosea 2:20", "v": [[2, 20, "I will even betroth thee unto me in faithfulness: and thou shalt know the LORD."]], "q": "God promises to be faithful to us. Being faithful means keeping your promises. What is one promise you can keep this week?", "act": "Pinky-promise one kind thing you will do for someone in the family before bedtime."}, {"r": "Hosea 2:19–20", "v": [[2, 19, "And I will betroth thee unto me for ever; yea, I will betroth thee unto me in righteousness, and in judgment, and in lovingkindness, and in mercies."], [2, 20, "I will even betroth thee unto me in faithfulness: and thou shalt know the LORD."]], "q": "God compares His promises with us to a marriage. Which words in these verses tell how He treats us?"}, {"r": "Hosea 2:19–20", "v": [[2, 19, "And I will betroth thee unto me for ever; yea, I will betroth thee unto me in righteousness, and in judgment, and in lovingkindness, and in mercies."], [2, 20, "I will even betroth thee unto me in faithfulness: and thou shalt know the LORD."]], "q": "“Betroth” means to promise to marry. Why might God describe His covenant with us like a marriage instead of a contract?"}, {"r": "Hosea 2:14–20", "v": [[2, 14, "Therefore, behold, I will allure her, and bring her into the wilderness, and speak comfortably unto her."], [2, 15, "And I will give her her vineyards from thence, and the valley of Achor for a door of hope: and she shall sing there, as in the days of her youth, and as in the day when she came up out of the land of Egypt."], [2, 16, "And it shall be at that day, saith the LORD, that thou shalt call me Ishi; and shalt call me no more Baali."], [2, 17, "For I will take away the names of Baalim out of her mouth, and they shall no more be remembered by their name."], [2, 18, "And in that day will I make a covenant for them with the beasts of the field, and with the fowls of heaven, and with the creeping things of the ground: and I will break the bow and the sword and the battle out of the earth, and will make them to lie down safely."], [2, 19, "And I will betroth thee unto me for ever; yea, I will betroth thee unto me in righteousness, and in judgment, and in lovingkindness, and in mercies."], [2, 20, "I will even betroth thee unto me in faithfulness: and thou shalt know the LORD."]], "q": "God promises to “speak comfortably” and turn a valley of trouble into “a door of hope” (verses 14–15). Where have you seen Him do that?"}], "ck": "God told Hosea to love his wife even when she left. That showed how God keeps loving His people.", "cf": "Hosea's own marriage, broken and then restored, is the backdrop here. God speaks of Israel as a bride He will win back, not with force but with faithfulness.", "wd": ["lovingkindness", "חֶסֶד", "chesed", "Hebrew", "loyal, covenant love: love that keeps its promises even when the other person doesn't."]}, {"d": "2026-11-18", "w": 47, "t": "Goodness that lasts", "a": [{"r": "Hosea 6:6", "v": [[6, 6, "For I desired mercy, and not sacrifice; and the knowledge of God more than burnt offerings."]], "q": "God says He wants “mercy,” which means being kind and forgiving. How can you be kind to someone today?", "act": "Pick one person to secretly do something kind for today."}, {"r": "Hosea 6:4, 6", "v": [[6, 4, "O Ephraim, what shall I do unto thee? O Judah, what shall I do unto thee? for your goodness is as a morning cloud, and as the early dew it goeth away."], [6, 6, "For I desired mercy, and not sacrifice; and the knowledge of God more than burnt offerings."]], "q": "Verse 4 says the people's goodness disappeared like morning dew. What would it look like for our goodness to last all day?"}, {"r": "Hosea 6:1–6", "v": [[6, 1, "Come, and let us return unto the LORD: for he hath torn, and he will heal us; he hath smitten, and he will bind us up."], [6, 2, "After two days will he revive us: in the third day he will raise us up, and we shall live in his sight."], [6, 3, "Then shall we know, if we follow on to know the LORD: his going forth is prepared as the morning; and he shall come unto us as the rain, as the latter and former rain unto the earth."], [6, 4, "O Ephraim, what shall I do unto thee? O Judah, what shall I do unto thee? for your goodness is as a morning cloud, and as the early dew it goeth away."], [6, 5, "Therefore have I hewed them by the prophets; I have slain them by the words of my mouth: and thy judgments are as the light that goeth forth."], [6, 6, "For I desired mercy, and not sacrifice; and the knowledge of God more than burnt offerings."]], "q": "In verse 6 God wants mercy and knowing Him more than offerings. What's the difference between doing religious things and actually knowing God?"}, {"r": "Hosea 6:1–6", "v": [[6, 1, "Come, and let us return unto the LORD: for he hath torn, and he will heal us; he hath smitten, and he will bind us up."], [6, 2, "After two days will he revive us: in the third day he will raise us up, and we shall live in his sight."], [6, 3, "Then shall we know, if we follow on to know the LORD: his going forth is prepared as the morning; and he shall come unto us as the rain, as the latter and former rain unto the earth."], [6, 4, "O Ephraim, what shall I do unto thee? O Judah, what shall I do unto thee? for your goodness is as a morning cloud, and as the early dew it goeth away."], [6, 5, "Therefore have I hewed them by the prophets; I have slain them by the words of my mouth: and thy judgments are as the light that goeth forth."], [6, 6, "For I desired mercy, and not sacrifice; and the knowledge of God more than burnt offerings."]], "q": "Their goodness was “as a morning cloud” (verse 4). What helps devotion last past Sunday in our home?"}], "ck": "In Hosea's day, people still made offerings at the temple, but they weren't really being kind or loving God.", "cf": "Israel kept up sacrifices and festivals while being dishonest and unkind. Dew and morning clouds in that land burn off fast in the sun, and Hosea says their goodness disappears just as quickly.", "wd": ["knowledge", "דַּעַת", "da'at", "Hebrew", "knowing someone personally, from experience, not just knowing facts about them."]}, {"d": "2026-11-19", "w": 47, "t": "Time to seek the Lord", "a": [{"r": "Hosea 10:12", "v": [[10, 12, "Sow to yourselves in righteousness, reap in mercy; break up your fallow ground: for it is time to seek the LORD, till he come and rain righteousness upon you."]], "q": "Hosea talks about planting seeds. What good things can we plant in our family, like kindness or prayer?", "act": "Draw a seed growing into a plant, and write on it one good thing you want to grow."}, {"r": "Hosea 10:12", "v": [[10, 12, "Sow to yourselves in righteousness, reap in mercy; break up your fallow ground: for it is time to seek the LORD, till he come and rain righteousness upon you."]], "q": "“Break up your fallow ground” means getting hard, unused dirt ready to plant. What helps soften our hearts so good things can grow?"}, {"r": "Hosea 10:12–13", "v": [[10, 12, "Sow to yourselves in righteousness, reap in mercy; break up your fallow ground: for it is time to seek the LORD, till he come and rain righteousness upon you."], [10, 13, "Ye have plowed wickedness, ye have reaped iniquity; ye have eaten the fruit of lies: because thou didst trust in thy way, in the multitude of thy mighty men."]], "q": "Hosea says “it is time to seek the LORD.” Why might now be the time, instead of later?"}, {"r": "Hosea 10:12–13", "v": [[10, 12, "Sow to yourselves in righteousness, reap in mercy; break up your fallow ground: for it is time to seek the LORD, till he come and rain righteousness upon you."], [10, 13, "Ye have plowed wickedness, ye have reaped iniquity; ye have eaten the fruit of lies: because thou didst trust in thy way, in the multitude of thy mighty men."]], "q": "Verse 13 says Israel trusted “in thy way” and reaped what they planted. What are we planting as a family right now, and what will it grow?"}], "ck": "Farmers in Israel had to plow hard, dry ground before the rain came, or nothing would grow.", "cf": "After the long dry summer, farmers broke up hardened ground so the autumn rains could soak in. Hosea uses farming to describe a heart that's ready for God.", "wd": ["rain", "יֹרֶה", "yoreh", "Hebrew", "the early rain, but the same word can also mean “to teach.” So God will “rain” and “teach” righteousness."]}, {"d": "2026-11-20", "w": 47, "t": "Jesus is our Savior", "a": [{"r": "Hosea 13:4", "v": [[13, 4, "Yet I am the LORD thy God from the land of Egypt, and thou shalt know no god but me: for there is no saviour beside me."]], "q": "God says, “There is no saviour beside me.” Jesus is our Savior. What has Jesus done for you?", "act": "Look at a picture of Jesus and have each person name one thing they love about Him."}, {"r": "Hosea 13:4, 14", "v": [[13, 4, "Yet I am the LORD thy God from the land of Egypt, and thou shalt know no god but me: for there is no saviour beside me."], [13, 14, "I will ransom them from the power of the grave; I will redeem them from death: O death, I will be thy plagues; O grave, I will be thy destruction: repentance shall be hid from mine eyes."]], "q": "In verse 14 God promises to “redeem them from death.” How did Jesus make that possible?"}, {"r": "Hosea 13:4, 9, 14", "v": [[13, 4, "Yet I am the LORD thy God from the land of Egypt, and thou shalt know no god but me: for there is no saviour beside me."], [13, 9, "O Israel, thou hast destroyed thyself; but in me is thine help."], [13, 14, "I will ransom them from the power of the grave; I will redeem them from death: O death, I will be thy plagues; O grave, I will be thy destruction: repentance shall be hid from mine eyes."]], "q": "Verse 9 says, “in me is thine help.” Where do people look for help instead of God, and why does Hosea say only He can save?"}, {"r": "Hosea 13:4–6, 9, 14", "v": [[13, 4, "Yet I am the LORD thy God from the land of Egypt, and thou shalt know no god but me: for there is no saviour beside me."], [13, 5, "I did know thee in the wilderness, in the land of great drought."], [13, 6, "According to their pasture, so were they filled; they were filled, and their heart was exalted; therefore have they forgotten me."], [13, 9, "O Israel, thou hast destroyed thyself; but in me is thine help."], [13, 14, "I will ransom them from the power of the grave; I will redeem them from death: O death, I will be thy plagues; O grave, I will be thy destruction: repentance shall be hid from mine eyes."]], "q": "Israel forgot God once they were full and comfortable (verse 6). Why is it easier to forget God when life is going well?"}], "ck": "Long ago God saved Israel from slavery in Egypt. Hosea reminds them that God is the one who saves.", "cf": "Hosea points back to the Exodus: the God who led Israel out of Egypt is their only Savior. Paul later quotes Hosea 13:14 when he writes about the Resurrection: “O death, where is thy sting?” (1 Corinthians 15:55).", "wd": ["redeem", "גָּאַל", "ga'al", "Hebrew", "to buy back. A redeemer was a close relative who paid to free a family member or win back lost land."]}, {"d": "2026-11-21", "w": 47, "t": "Turning with all our heart", "a": [{"r": "Joel 2:13", "v": [[2, 13, "And rend your heart, and not your garments, and turn unto the LORD your God: for he is gracious and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness, and repenteth him of the evil."]], "q": "God is “slow to anger, and of great kindness.” Who do you know who is slow to get angry? How can we be like that?", "act": "Practice taking three slow breaths together, something to do when you start to feel angry."}, {"r": "Joel 2:12–13", "v": [[2, 12, "Therefore also now, saith the LORD, turn ye even to me with all your heart, and with fasting, and with weeping, and with mourning:"], [2, 13, "And rend your heart, and not your garments, and turn unto the LORD your God: for he is gracious and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness, and repenteth him of the evil."]], "q": "People used to tear their clothes to show they were sorry. Why does God ask us to “rend your heart, and not your garments”?"}, {"r": "Joel 2:12–13", "v": [[2, 12, "Therefore also now, saith the LORD, turn ye even to me with all your heart, and with fasting, and with weeping, and with mourning:"], [2, 13, "And rend your heart, and not your garments, and turn unto the LORD your God: for he is gracious and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness, and repenteth him of the evil."]], "q": "What's the difference between looking sorry and actually turning back to God “with all your heart”?"}, {"r": "Joel 2:12–13", "v": [[2, 12, "Therefore also now, saith the LORD, turn ye even to me with all your heart, and with fasting, and with weeping, and with mourning:"], [2, 13, "And rend your heart, and not your garments, and turn unto the LORD your God: for he is gracious and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness, and repenteth him of the evil."]], "q": "Joel describes God as “gracious and merciful, slow to anger.” How does knowing that change the way we repent?"}], "ck": "A huge swarm of locusts, like giant grasshoppers, ate all the crops. The prophet Joel told the people to turn back to God.", "cf": "Joel 1 describes a locust plague that stripped the land bare. Joel uses it as a wake-up call. In that culture, people tore their clothes to show grief, but God asks for more than an outward show.", "wd": ["rend", "קָרַע", "qara", "Hebrew", "to tear. People tore their clothes in sorrow; God asks them to tear open their hearts instead."]}, {"d": "2026-11-22", "w": 47, "t": "The Spirit for everyone", "a": [{"r": "Joel 2:28", "v": [[2, 28, "And it shall come to pass afterward, that I will pour out my spirit upon all flesh; and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy, your old men shall dream dreams, your young men shall see visions:"]], "q": "God promised to pour out His Spirit, the Holy Ghost, on everyone, even sons and daughters. How does the Holy Ghost help you?", "act": "Slowly pour water into a cup while you talk about how the Holy Ghost can fill us with peace."}, {"r": "Joel 2:28–29", "v": [[2, 28, "And it shall come to pass afterward, that I will pour out my spirit upon all flesh; and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy, your old men shall dream dreams, your young men shall see visions:"], [2, 29, "And also upon the servants and upon the handmaids in those days will I pour out my spirit."]], "q": "Joel says young and old, sons and daughters, will all receive the Spirit. What does that tell you about who God speaks to?"}, {"r": "Joel 2:28–32", "v": [[2, 28, "And it shall come to pass afterward, that I will pour out my spirit upon all flesh; and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy, your old men shall dream dreams, your young men shall see visions:"], [2, 29, "And also upon the servants and upon the handmaids in those days will I pour out my spirit."], [2, 30, "And I will shew wonders in the heavens and in the earth, blood, and fire, and pillars of smoke."], [2, 31, "The sun shall be turned into darkness, and the moon into blood, before the great and the terrible day of the LORD come."], [2, 32, "And it shall come to pass, that whosoever shall call on the name of the LORD shall be delivered: for in mount Zion and in Jerusalem shall be deliverance, as the LORD hath said, and in the remnant whom the LORD shall call."]], "q": "Peter quoted this prophecy on the day of Pentecost (Acts 2:16–18). When have you seen the Spirit come to ordinary people?"}, {"r": "Joel 2:28–32", "v": [[2, 28, "And it shall come to pass afterward, that I will pour out my spirit upon all flesh; and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy, your old men shall dream dreams, your young men shall see visions:"], [2, 29, "And also upon the servants and upon the handmaids in those days will I pour out my spirit."], [2, 30, "And I will shew wonders in the heavens and in the earth, blood, and fire, and pillars of smoke."], [2, 31, "The sun shall be turned into darkness, and the moon into blood, before the great and the terrible day of the LORD come."], [2, 32, "And it shall come to pass, that whosoever shall call on the name of the LORD shall be delivered: for in mount Zion and in Jerusalem shall be deliverance, as the LORD hath said, and in the remnant whom the LORD shall call."]], "q": "“Whosoever shall call on the name of the LORD shall be delivered” (verse 32). What does calling on His name look like in daily life?"}], "ck": "After the locusts, God promised good things would come back, and that His Spirit would come to everyone.", "cf": "After promising to restore what the locusts ate (2:25), Joel looks further ahead. Peter quoted this prophecy on the day of Pentecost (Acts 2:16–21), and the angel Moroni quoted it to Joseph Smith, saying it was about to be fulfilled (Joseph Smith—History 1:41).", "wd": ["pour out", "שָׁפַךְ", "shaphak", "Hebrew", "to pour out generously, the way you'd empty a full pitcher, not a few drops."]}, {"d": "2026-11-23", "w": 48, "t": "Prophets share God's messages", "a": [{"r": "Amos 3:7", "v": [[3, 7, "Surely the Lord GOD will do nothing, but he revealeth his secret unto his servants the prophets."]], "q": "God tells His plans to prophets. Who is the prophet today, and what has he taught us?", "act": "Play a quick game of telephone, then talk about why we listen to the prophet instead of what people pass around."}, {"r": "Amos 3:7–8", "v": [[3, 7, "Surely the Lord GOD will do nothing, but he revealeth his secret unto his servants the prophets."], [3, 8, "The lion hath roared, who will not fear? the Lord GOD hath spoken, who can but prophesy?"]], "q": "Amos asks, “The Lord GOD hath spoken, who can but prophesy?” Why would a prophet feel he has to share God's message?"}, {"r": "Amos 3:3–8", "v": [[3, 3, "Can two walk together, except they be agreed?"], [3, 4, "Will a lion roar in the forest, when he hath no prey? will a young lion cry out of his den, if he have taken nothing?"], [3, 5, "Can a bird fall in a snare upon the earth, where no gin is for him? shall one take up a snare from the earth, and have taken nothing at all?"], [3, 6, "Shall a trumpet be blown in the city, and the people not be afraid? shall there be evil in a city, and the LORD hath not done it?"], [3, 7, "Surely the Lord GOD will do nothing, but he revealeth his secret unto his servants the prophets."], [3, 8, "The lion hath roared, who will not fear? the Lord GOD hath spoken, who can but prophesy?"]], "q": "Amos lists cause-and-effect questions, like a lion roaring because it has caught something. What point is he making about why prophets speak?"}, {"r": "Amos 3:3–8", "v": [[3, 3, "Can two walk together, except they be agreed?"], [3, 4, "Will a lion roar in the forest, when he hath no prey? will a young lion cry out of his den, if he have taken nothing?"], [3, 5, "Can a bird fall in a snare upon the earth, where no gin is for him? shall one take up a snare from the earth, and have taken nothing at all?"], [3, 6, "Shall a trumpet be blown in the city, and the people not be afraid? shall there be evil in a city, and the LORD hath not done it?"], [3, 7, "Surely the Lord GOD will do nothing, but he revealeth his secret unto his servants the prophets."], [3, 8, "The lion hath roared, who will not fear? the Lord GOD hath spoken, who can but prophesy?"]], "q": "“Can two walk together, except they be agreed?” (verse 3). What does walking with God ask of us?"}], "ck": "Amos was a shepherd who also took care of fig trees. God called him to be a prophet.", "cf": "Amos came from Tekoa in Judah and worked as a herdsman and fig farmer (Amos 1:1; 7:14). Around 760 BC God sent him north to Israel during a time of wealth and comfort, when few wanted to hear a warning.", "wd": ["secret", "סוֹד", "sod", "Hebrew", "a council, or the private plans shared in it. Prophets are invited into God's council."]}, {"d": "2026-11-24", "w": 48, "t": "Seek the Lord and live", "a": [{"r": "Amos 5:4", "v": [[5, 4, "For thus saith the LORD unto the house of Israel, Seek ye me, and ye shall live:"]], "q": "God says, “Seek ye me, and ye shall live.” Seek means look for. Where can we look for God?", "act": "Hide a picture of Jesus and let the children find it. Then talk about ways we can look for Him."}, {"r": "Amos 5:4, 14", "v": [[5, 4, "For thus saith the LORD unto the house of Israel, Seek ye me, and ye shall live:"], [5, 14, "Seek good, and not evil, that ye may live: and so the LORD, the God of hosts, shall be with you, as ye have spoken."]], "q": "“Seek good, and not evil.” What is one good thing our family could seek this week?"}, {"r": "Amos 5:4–8, 14–15", "v": [[5, 4, "For thus saith the LORD unto the house of Israel, Seek ye me, and ye shall live:"], [5, 5, "But seek not Beth-el, nor enter into Gilgal, and pass not to Beer-sheba: for Gilgal shall surely go into captivity, and Beth-el shall come to nought."], [5, 6, "Seek the LORD, and ye shall live; lest he break out like fire in the house of Joseph, and devour it, and there be none to quench it in Beth-el."], [5, 7, "Ye who turn judgment to wormwood, and leave off righteousness in the earth,"], [5, 8, "Seek him that maketh the seven stars and Orion, and turneth the shadow of death into the morning, and maketh the day dark with night: that calleth for the waters of the sea, and poureth them out upon the face of the earth: The LORD is his name:"], [5, 14, "Seek good, and not evil, that ye may live: and so the LORD, the God of hosts, shall be with you, as ye have spoken."], [5, 15, "Hate the evil, and love the good, and establish judgment in the gate: it may be that the LORD God of hosts will be gracious unto the remnant of Joseph."]], "q": "In verse 8, Amos points to the God who made the stars. How does remembering how great God is help us seek Him?"}, {"r": "Amos 5:4–15", "v": [[5, 4, "For thus saith the LORD unto the house of Israel, Seek ye me, and ye shall live:"], [5, 5, "But seek not Beth-el, nor enter into Gilgal, and pass not to Beer-sheba: for Gilgal shall surely go into captivity, and Beth-el shall come to nought."], [5, 6, "Seek the LORD, and ye shall live; lest he break out like fire in the house of Joseph, and devour it, and there be none to quench it in Beth-el."], [5, 7, "Ye who turn judgment to wormwood, and leave off righteousness in the earth,"], [5, 8, "Seek him that maketh the seven stars and Orion, and turneth the shadow of death into the morning, and maketh the day dark with night: that calleth for the waters of the sea, and poureth them out upon the face of the earth: The LORD is his name:"], [5, 9, "That strengtheneth the spoiled against the strong, so that the spoiled shall come against the fortress."], [5, 10, "They hate him that rebuketh in the gate, and they abhor him that speaketh uprightly."], [5, 11, "Forasmuch therefore as your treading is upon the poor, and ye take from him burdens of wheat: ye have built houses of hewn stone, but ye shall not dwell in them; ye have planted pleasant vineyards, but ye shall not drink wine of them."], [5, 12, "For I know your manifold transgressions and your mighty sins: they afflict the just, they take a bribe, and they turn aside the poor in the gate from their right."], [5, 13, "Therefore the prudent shall keep silence in that time; for it is an evil time."], [5, 14, "Seek good, and not evil, that ye may live: and so the LORD, the God of hosts, shall be with you, as ye have spoken."], [5, 15, "Hate the evil, and love the good, and establish judgment in the gate: it may be that the LORD God of hosts will be gracious unto the remnant of Joseph."]], "q": "Amos ties seeking God to treating the poor fairly (verses 11–12, 15). Why are worship and how we treat others connected?"}], "ck": "People in Israel were going to special places to worship idols. Amos told them to look for God instead.", "cf": "Bethel, Gilgal and Beersheba were famous shrines, and at Bethel Israel worshiped a golden calf. Amos says going to the right place means nothing if you aren't seeking the Lord and treating people justly.", "wd": ["seek", "דָּרַשׁ", "darash", "Hebrew", "to search carefully, to ask and keep asking, the way you'd look for something you really need."]}, {"d": "2026-11-25", "w": 48, "t": "Hungry for God's word", "a": [{"r": "Amos 8:11", "v": [[8, 11, "Behold, the days come, saith the Lord GOD, that I will send a famine in the land, not a famine of bread, nor a thirst for water, but of hearing the words of the LORD:"]], "q": "Amos talks about being hungry, not for food but for God's words. What words of God do you love to hear?", "act": "Share a favorite snack, and let each person share a favorite scripture or song while you eat."}, {"r": "Amos 8:11–12", "v": [[8, 11, "Behold, the days come, saith the Lord GOD, that I will send a famine in the land, not a famine of bread, nor a thirst for water, but of hearing the words of the LORD:"], [8, 12, "And they shall wander from sea to sea, and from the north even to the east, they shall run to and fro to seek the word of the LORD, and shall not find it."]], "q": "What would it be like if no one could find the scriptures or a prophet? How are we blessed to have them?"}, {"r": "Amos 8:11–12", "v": [[8, 11, "Behold, the days come, saith the Lord GOD, that I will send a famine in the land, not a famine of bread, nor a thirst for water, but of hearing the words of the LORD:"], [8, 12, "And they shall wander from sea to sea, and from the north even to the east, they shall run to and fro to seek the word of the LORD, and shall not find it."]], "q": "People “run to and fro” looking for God's word and can't find it. How did the Restoration end that kind of famine?"}, {"r": "Amos 8:11–12", "v": [[8, 11, "Behold, the days come, saith the Lord GOD, that I will send a famine in the land, not a famine of bread, nor a thirst for water, but of hearing the words of the LORD:"], [8, 12, "And they shall wander from sea to sea, and from the north even to the east, they shall run to and fro to seek the word of the LORD, and shall not find it."]], "q": "Where do you see spiritual hunger in the people around you, and how can our family share what fills it?"}], "ck": "Israel was rich and had plenty of food, but they weren't listening to God's prophets.", "cf": "Amos spoke during good economic times. He warns that a day would come when people would want God's word and not be able to find it, a famine worse than running out of bread.", "wd": ["famine", "רָעָב", "ra'av", "Hebrew", "hunger or starvation. The same word is used for real famines of food, which makes Amos's picture hit hard."]}, {"d": "2026-11-26", "w": 48, "t": "Helping our ancestors", "a": [{"r": "Obadiah 1:21", "v": [[1, 21, "And saviours shall come up on mount Zion to judge the mount of Esau; and the kingdom shall be the LORD’s."]], "q": "Prophets have taught that when we do temple work for people who have died, we help them like Jesus helps us. Who in our family history would you like to learn about?", "act": "Look at a photo of a grandparent or ancestor and share one story about them."}, {"r": "Obadiah 1:21", "v": [[1, 21, "And saviours shall come up on mount Zion to judge the mount of Esau; and the kingdom shall be the LORD’s."]], "q": "“Saviours shall come up on mount Zion.” How is doing temple work for someone who died like what Jesus does for us?"}, {"r": "Obadiah 1:21", "v": [[1, 21, "And saviours shall come up on mount Zion to judge the mount of Esau; and the kingdom shall be the LORD’s."]], "q": "How can family history or doing baptisms for the dead let you help save someone?"}, {"r": "Obadiah 1:21", "v": [[1, 21, "And saviours shall come up on mount Zion to judge the mount of Esau; and the kingdom shall be the LORD’s."]], "q": "On Thanksgiving: which ancestors are you grateful for today, and what do you know about their lives?"}], "ck": "Obadiah is the shortest book in the Old Testament. It's only one chapter long!", "cf": "Obadiah's 21 verses are about Edom, the descendants of Esau, who cheered when Jerusalem was attacked. The book ends with hope: saviors on Mount Zion and the kingdom belonging to the Lord.", "wd": ["saviours", "מוֹשִׁעִים", "moshi'im", "Hebrew", "“those who save,” from yasha, “to save.” The names Joshua and Jesus come from the same root."]}, {"d": "2026-11-27", "w": 48, "t": "Jonah runs away", "a": [{"r": "Jonah 1:3, 17", "v": [[1, 3, "But Jonah rose up to flee unto Tarshish from the presence of the LORD, and went down to Joppa; and he found a ship going to Tarshish: so he paid the fare thereof, and went down into it, to go with them unto Tarshish from the presence of the LORD."], [1, 17, "Now the LORD had prepared a great fish to swallow up Jonah. And Jonah was in the belly of the fish three days and three nights."]], "q": "God asked Jonah to go to Nineveh, but Jonah went the other way. What happened next?", "act": "Act out the story: one person is the ship, one the storm, and one the big fish."}, {"r": "Jonah 1:1–4, 15–17", "v": [[1, 1, "Now the word of the LORD came unto Jonah the son of Amittai, saying,"], [1, 2, "Arise, go to Nineveh, that great city, and cry against it; for their wickedness is come up before me."], [1, 3, "But Jonah rose up to flee unto Tarshish from the presence of the LORD, and went down to Joppa; and he found a ship going to Tarshish: so he paid the fare thereof, and went down into it, to go with them unto Tarshish from the presence of the LORD."], [1, 4, "But the LORD sent out a great wind into the sea, and there was a mighty tempest in the sea, so that the ship was like to be broken."], [1, 15, "So they took up Jonah, and cast him forth into the sea: and the sea ceased from her raging."], [1, 16, "Then the men feared the LORD exceedingly, and offered a sacrifice unto the LORD, and made vows."], [1, 17, "Now the LORD had prepared a great fish to swallow up Jonah. And Jonah was in the belly of the fish three days and three nights."]], "q": "Why do you think Jonah didn't want to go? Have you ever not wanted to do something God asked?"}, {"r": "Jonah 1:1–17", "v": [[1, 1, "Now the word of the LORD came unto Jonah the son of Amittai, saying,"], [1, 2, "Arise, go to Nineveh, that great city, and cry against it; for their wickedness is come up before me."], [1, 3, "But Jonah rose up to flee unto Tarshish from the presence of the LORD, and went down to Joppa; and he found a ship going to Tarshish: so he paid the fare thereof, and went down into it, to go with them unto Tarshish from the presence of the LORD."], [1, 4, "But the LORD sent out a great wind into the sea, and there was a mighty tempest in the sea, so that the ship was like to be broken."], [1, 5, "Then the mariners were afraid, and cried every man unto his god, and cast forth the wares that were in the ship into the sea, to lighten it of them. But Jonah was gone down into the sides of the ship; and he lay, and was fast asleep."], [1, 6, "So the shipmaster came to him, and said unto him, What meanest thou, O sleeper? arise, call upon thy God, if so be that God will think upon us, that we perish not."], [1, 7, "And they said every one to his fellow, Come, and let us cast lots, that we may know for whose cause this evil is upon us. So they cast lots, and the lot fell upon Jonah."], [1, 8, "Then said they unto him, Tell us, we pray thee, for whose cause this evil is upon us; What is thine occupation? and whence comest thou? what is thy country? and of what people art thou?"], [1, 9, "And he said unto them, I am an Hebrew; and I fear the LORD, the God of heaven, which hath made the sea and the dry land."], [1, 10, "Then were the men exceedingly afraid, and said unto him, Why hast thou done this? For the men knew that he fled from the presence of the LORD, because he had told them."], [1, 11, "Then said they unto him, What shall we do unto thee, that the sea may be calm unto us? for the sea wrought, and was tempestuous."], [1, 12, "And he said unto them, Take me up, and cast me forth into the sea; so shall the sea be calm unto you: for I know that for my sake this great tempest is upon you."], [1, 13, "Nevertheless the men rowed hard to bring it to the land; but they could not: for the sea wrought, and was tempestuous against them."], [1, 14, "Wherefore they cried unto the LORD, and said, We beseech thee, O LORD, we beseech thee, let us not perish for this man’s life, and lay not upon us innocent blood: for thou, O LORD, hast done as it pleased thee."], [1, 15, "So they took up Jonah, and cast him forth into the sea: and the sea ceased from her raging."], [1, 16, "Then the men feared the LORD exceedingly, and offered a sacrifice unto the LORD, and made vows."], [1, 17, "Now the LORD had prepared a great fish to swallow up Jonah. And Jonah was in the belly of the fish three days and three nights."]], "q": "The sailors prayed while Jonah slept (verses 5–6). What do you notice about how the sailors act compared with Jonah?"}, {"r": "Jonah 1:1–17", "v": [[1, 1, "Now the word of the LORD came unto Jonah the son of Amittai, saying,"], [1, 2, "Arise, go to Nineveh, that great city, and cry against it; for their wickedness is come up before me."], [1, 3, "But Jonah rose up to flee unto Tarshish from the presence of the LORD, and went down to Joppa; and he found a ship going to Tarshish: so he paid the fare thereof, and went down into it, to go with them unto Tarshish from the presence of the LORD."], [1, 4, "But the LORD sent out a great wind into the sea, and there was a mighty tempest in the sea, so that the ship was like to be broken."], [1, 5, "Then the mariners were afraid, and cried every man unto his god, and cast forth the wares that were in the ship into the sea, to lighten it of them. But Jonah was gone down into the sides of the ship; and he lay, and was fast asleep."], [1, 6, "So the shipmaster came to him, and said unto him, What meanest thou, O sleeper? arise, call upon thy God, if so be that God will think upon us, that we perish not."], [1, 7, "And they said every one to his fellow, Come, and let us cast lots, that we may know for whose cause this evil is upon us. So they cast lots, and the lot fell upon Jonah."], [1, 8, "Then said they unto him, Tell us, we pray thee, for whose cause this evil is upon us; What is thine occupation? and whence comest thou? what is thy country? and of what people art thou?"], [1, 9, "And he said unto them, I am an Hebrew; and I fear the LORD, the God of heaven, which hath made the sea and the dry land."], [1, 10, "Then were the men exceedingly afraid, and said unto him, Why hast thou done this? For the men knew that he fled from the presence of the LORD, because he had told them."], [1, 11, "Then said they unto him, What shall we do unto thee, that the sea may be calm unto us? for the sea wrought, and was tempestuous."], [1, 12, "And he said unto them, Take me up, and cast me forth into the sea; so shall the sea be calm unto you: for I know that for my sake this great tempest is upon you."], [1, 13, "Nevertheless the men rowed hard to bring it to the land; but they could not: for the sea wrought, and was tempestuous against them."], [1, 14, "Wherefore they cried unto the LORD, and said, We beseech thee, O LORD, we beseech thee, let us not perish for this man’s life, and lay not upon us innocent blood: for thou, O LORD, hast done as it pleased thee."], [1, 15, "So they took up Jonah, and cast him forth into the sea: and the sea ceased from her raging."], [1, 16, "Then the men feared the LORD exceedingly, and offered a sacrifice unto the LORD, and made vows."], [1, 17, "Now the LORD had prepared a great fish to swallow up Jonah. And Jonah was in the belly of the fish three days and three nights."]], "q": "Jonah “rose up to flee… from the presence of the LORD” (verse 3). Why do we sometimes try to run from what God asks?"}], "ck": "Nineveh was a big city full of people who were enemies of Israel. God asked Jonah to go warn them.", "cf": "Nineveh was the capital of Assyria, Israel's feared and violent enemy. Instead of heading east, Jonah went to the port of Joppa and sailed for Tarshish, probably in Spain, about as far the other way as he could go.", "wd": ["great fish", "דָּג גָּדוֹל", "dag gadol", "Hebrew", "simply “a great fish.” The Hebrew doesn't say what kind; “whale” comes from later translations."]}, {"d": "2026-11-28", "w": 48, "t": "A second chance", "a": [{"r": "Jonah 2:1; 3:5", "v": [[2, 1, "Then Jonah prayed unto the LORD his God out of the fish’s belly,"], [3, 5, "So the people of Nineveh believed God, and proclaimed a fast, and put on sackcloth, from the greatest of them even to the least of them."]], "q": "Jonah prayed inside the fish, and God heard him. Can God hear our prayers anywhere?", "act": "Whisper a short prayer together, then talk about how God hears even quiet prayers."}, {"r": "Jonah 2:1–2, 10; 3:1–5", "v": [[2, 1, "Then Jonah prayed unto the LORD his God out of the fish’s belly,"], [2, 2, "And said, I cried by reason of mine affliction unto the LORD, and he heard me; out of the belly of hell cried I, and thou heardest my voice."], [2, 10, "And the LORD spake unto the fish, and it vomited out Jonah upon the dry land."], [3, 1, "And the word of the LORD came unto Jonah the second time, saying,"], [3, 2, "Arise, go unto Nineveh, that great city, and preach unto it the preaching that I bid thee."], [3, 3, "So Jonah arose, and went unto Nineveh, according to the word of the LORD. Now Nineveh was an exceeding great city of three days’ journey."], [3, 4, "And Jonah began to enter into the city a day’s journey, and he cried, and said, Yet forty days, and Nineveh shall be overthrown."], [3, 5, "So the people of Nineveh believed God, and proclaimed a fast, and put on sackcloth, from the greatest of them even to the least of them."]], "q": "God gave Jonah a second chance (3:1). How did the people of Nineveh use their second chance?"}, {"r": "Jonah 2:1–10; 3:1–5, 10", "v": [[2, 1, "Then Jonah prayed unto the LORD his God out of the fish’s belly,"], [2, 2, "And said, I cried by reason of mine affliction unto the LORD, and he heard me; out of the belly of hell cried I, and thou heardest my voice."], [2, 3, "For thou hadst cast me into the deep, in the midst of the seas; and the floods compassed me about: all thy billows and thy waves passed over me."], [2, 4, "Then I said, I am cast out of thy sight; yet I will look again toward thy holy temple."], [2, 5, "The waters compassed me about, even to the soul: the depth closed me round about, the weeds were wrapped about my head."], [2, 6, "I went down to the bottoms of the mountains; the earth with her bars was about me for ever: yet hast thou brought up my life from corruption, O LORD my God."], [2, 7, "When my soul fainted within me I remembered the LORD: and my prayer came in unto thee, into thine holy temple."], [2, 8, "They that observe lying vanities forsake their own mercy."], [2, 9, "But I will sacrifice unto thee with the voice of thanksgiving; I will pay that that I have vowed. Salvation is of the LORD."], [2, 10, "And the LORD spake unto the fish, and it vomited out Jonah upon the dry land."], [3, 1, "And the word of the LORD came unto Jonah the second time, saying,"], [3, 2, "Arise, go unto Nineveh, that great city, and preach unto it the preaching that I bid thee."], [3, 3, "So Jonah arose, and went unto Nineveh, according to the word of the LORD. Now Nineveh was an exceeding great city of three days’ journey."], [3, 4, "And Jonah began to enter into the city a day’s journey, and he cried, and said, Yet forty days, and Nineveh shall be overthrown."], [3, 5, "So the people of Nineveh believed God, and proclaimed a fast, and put on sackcloth, from the greatest of them even to the least of them."], [3, 10, "And God saw their works, that they turned from their evil way; and God repented of the evil, that he had said that he would do unto them; and he did it not."]], "q": "“The word of the LORD came unto Jonah the second time” (3:1). What does this story teach about second chances?"}, {"r": "Jonah 2:1–10; 3:1–10", "v": [[2, 1, "Then Jonah prayed unto the LORD his God out of the fish’s belly,"], [2, 2, "And said, I cried by reason of mine affliction unto the LORD, and he heard me; out of the belly of hell cried I, and thou heardest my voice."], [2, 3, "For thou hadst cast me into the deep, in the midst of the seas; and the floods compassed me about: all thy billows and thy waves passed over me."], [2, 4, "Then I said, I am cast out of thy sight; yet I will look again toward thy holy temple."], [2, 5, "The waters compassed me about, even to the soul: the depth closed me round about, the weeds were wrapped about my head."], [2, 6, "I went down to the bottoms of the mountains; the earth with her bars was about me for ever: yet hast thou brought up my life from corruption, O LORD my God."], [2, 7, "When my soul fainted within me I remembered the LORD: and my prayer came in unto thee, into thine holy temple."], [2, 8, "They that observe lying vanities forsake their own mercy."], [2, 9, "But I will sacrifice unto thee with the voice of thanksgiving; I will pay that that I have vowed. Salvation is of the LORD."], [2, 10, "And the LORD spake unto the fish, and it vomited out Jonah upon the dry land."], [3, 1, "And the word of the LORD came unto Jonah the second time, saying,"], [3, 2, "Arise, go unto Nineveh, that great city, and preach unto it the preaching that I bid thee."], [3, 3, "So Jonah arose, and went unto Nineveh, according to the word of the LORD. Now Nineveh was an exceeding great city of three days’ journey."], [3, 4, "And Jonah began to enter into the city a day’s journey, and he cried, and said, Yet forty days, and Nineveh shall be overthrown."], [3, 5, "So the people of Nineveh believed God, and proclaimed a fast, and put on sackcloth, from the greatest of them even to the least of them."], [3, 6, "For word came unto the king of Nineveh, and he arose from his throne, and he laid his robe from him, and covered him with sackcloth, and sat in ashes."], [3, 7, "And he caused it to be proclaimed and published through Nineveh by the decree of the king and his nobles, saying, Let neither man nor beast, herd nor flock, taste any thing: let them not feed, nor drink water:"], [3, 8, "But let man and beast be covered with sackcloth, and cry mightily unto God: yea, let them turn every one from his evil way, and from the violence that is in their hands."], [3, 9, "Who can tell if God will turn and repent, and turn away from his fierce anger, that we perish not?"], [3, 10, "And God saw their works, that they turned from their evil way; and God repented of the evil, that he had said that he would do unto them; and he did it not."]], "q": "Jonah remembered the Lord “when my soul fainted within me” (2:7). What does his prayer teach about turning to God when we're at our lowest?"}], "ck": "Jonah was inside the fish for three days. Then he finally went to Nineveh, and the whole city said sorry to God.", "cf": "Jesus called this “the sign of the prophet Jonas”: three days and nights in the fish pointed to His own death and Resurrection (Matthew 12:39–41). Nineveh's repentance is total, from the king down to the animals.", "wd": ["sackcloth", "שַׂק", "saq", "Hebrew", "rough cloth made of goat hair, worn to show sorrow and humility."]}, {"d": "2026-11-29", "w": 48, "t": "God's mercy for everyone", "a": [{"r": "Jonah 4:2", "v": [[4, 2, "And he prayed unto the LORD, and said, I pray thee, O LORD, was not this my saying, when I was yet in my country? Therefore I fled before unto Tarshish: for I knew that thou art a gracious God, and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness, and repentest thee of the evil."]], "q": "Jonah said God is “gracious… and merciful… and of great kindness.” How has God been kind to our family?", "act": "Make a list together of ways God has been kind to your family this week."}, {"r": "Jonah 4:1–4", "v": [[4, 1, "But it displeased Jonah exceedingly, and he was very angry."], [4, 2, "And he prayed unto the LORD, and said, I pray thee, O LORD, was not this my saying, when I was yet in my country? Therefore I fled before unto Tarshish: for I knew that thou art a gracious God, and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness, and repentest thee of the evil."], [4, 3, "Therefore now, O LORD, take, I beseech thee, my life from me; for it is better for me to die than to live."], [4, 4, "Then said the LORD, Doest thou well to be angry?"]], "q": "Jonah was angry that God forgave Nineveh. Why can it be hard to be happy when someone else is forgiven?"}, {"r": "Jonah 4:1–11", "v": [[4, 1, "But it displeased Jonah exceedingly, and he was very angry."], [4, 2, "And he prayed unto the LORD, and said, I pray thee, O LORD, was not this my saying, when I was yet in my country? Therefore I fled before unto Tarshish: for I knew that thou art a gracious God, and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness, and repentest thee of the evil."], [4, 3, "Therefore now, O LORD, take, I beseech thee, my life from me; for it is better for me to die than to live."], [4, 4, "Then said the LORD, Doest thou well to be angry?"], [4, 5, "So Jonah went out of the city, and sat on the east side of the city, and there made him a booth, and sat under it in the shadow, till he might see what would become of the city."], [4, 6, "And the LORD God prepared a gourd, and made it to come up over Jonah, that it might be a shadow over his head, to deliver him from his grief. So Jonah was exceeding glad of the gourd."], [4, 7, "But God prepared a worm when the morning rose the next day, and it smote the gourd that it withered."], [4, 8, "And it came to pass, when the sun did arise, that God prepared a vehement east wind; and the sun beat upon the head of Jonah, that he fainted, and wished in himself to die, and said, It is better for me to die than to live."], [4, 9, "And God said to Jonah, Doest thou well to be angry for the gourd? And he said, I do well to be angry, even unto death."], [4, 10, "Then said the LORD, Thou hast had pity on the gourd, for the which thou hast not laboured, neither madest it grow; which came up in a night, and perished in a night:"], [4, 11, "And should not I spare Nineveh, that great city, wherein are more than sixscore thousand persons that cannot discern between their right hand and their left hand; and also much cattle?"]], "q": "God used a plant to teach Jonah (verses 6–11). What was the lesson?"}, {"r": "Jonah 4:1–11", "v": [[4, 1, "But it displeased Jonah exceedingly, and he was very angry."], [4, 2, "And he prayed unto the LORD, and said, I pray thee, O LORD, was not this my saying, when I was yet in my country? Therefore I fled before unto Tarshish: for I knew that thou art a gracious God, and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness, and repentest thee of the evil."], [4, 3, "Therefore now, O LORD, take, I beseech thee, my life from me; for it is better for me to die than to live."], [4, 4, "Then said the LORD, Doest thou well to be angry?"], [4, 5, "So Jonah went out of the city, and sat on the east side of the city, and there made him a booth, and sat under it in the shadow, till he might see what would become of the city."], [4, 6, "And the LORD God prepared a gourd, and made it to come up over Jonah, that it might be a shadow over his head, to deliver him from his grief. So Jonah was exceeding glad of the gourd."], [4, 7, "But God prepared a worm when the morning rose the next day, and it smote the gourd that it withered."], [4, 8, "And it came to pass, when the sun did arise, that God prepared a vehement east wind; and the sun beat upon the head of Jonah, that he fainted, and wished in himself to die, and said, It is better for me to die than to live."], [4, 9, "And God said to Jonah, Doest thou well to be angry for the gourd? And he said, I do well to be angry, even unto death."], [4, 10, "Then said the LORD, Thou hast had pity on the gourd, for the which thou hast not laboured, neither madest it grow; which came up in a night, and perished in a night:"], [4, 11, "And should not I spare Nineveh, that great city, wherein are more than sixscore thousand persons that cannot discern between their right hand and their left hand; and also much cattle?"]], "q": "The book ends with God's question (verse 11). How would you answer it, and who might be our Nineveh?"}], "ck": "Jonah was upset that God forgave Nineveh. God used a plant to teach him a lesson.", "cf": "The book ends with a question, not an answer. God points to 120,000 people who “cannot discern between their right hand and their left,” likely meaning children or people who didn't know better.", "wd": ["gourd", "קִיקָיוֹן", "qiqayon", "Hebrew", "probably a castor-oil plant, a fast-growing plant with big leaves that gives quick shade."]}];
